{
  "FormatVersion": 3,
  "Id": "ae28dc10-527b-5b7e-8f97-099aacc5aa79",
  "Description": "Balls fall onto a shape that exists only as an SDF field.\n\nA [BoxSDF] with a [SphereSDF] cut out of it is a field: a formula on the GPU, with no mesh. [GridPoints] lays a 3D grid of points over it, [SampleFieldPoints] writes the field's distance into every point and [ReadPointsFromGpu] brings them to the CPU. [SdfPointsToGeometry] extracts the surface, and that geometry is the Geometry of a static [RigidBody]. Any field takes the same route, including a volume through [VdbToSdfField].\n\nAKA: sdf collider, field collider, raymarch, implicit surface, signed distance\n\nTips\n- The point grid sets the detail of the collider. Its counts times Size must cover the shape with a little room to spare.\n- The field is sampled once. For a field that changes, turn on UpdateContinuously on [ReadPointsFromGpu]; the collider is rebuilt whenever its surface changes.\n- What you see is the collider's geometry. Add a [RaymarchField] on the same field to see the field itself.",
